6. Behaviour reproduced from other libraries
==============================================================================

R5's results depend on details of libraries it calls. To return the same
results, peabiru has its own Rust code, written for this project, that
behaves as those libraries do in the cases R5 exercises. None of their
source code is included (other than the table in section 3); they are
named here for transparency.

  java.util.PriorityQueue (OpenJDK)
      street/router.rs (JavaHeap): a binary heap whose sift-up and sift-down
      steps leave equal keys in the order java.util.PriorityQueue does.
  java.util.Objects.hash, java.lang.Double.hashCode (OpenJDK)
      jcompat.rs: the 31-multiplier hash of three values, which R5 uses to
      seed the random number generator.
  Trove 3, gnu.trove.map.hash.TIntIntHashMap and TIntObjectHashMap
      jcompat.rs (TroveIntKeys): the placement of int keys in the
      open-addressing table (hash, double-hashing probe, prime capacities,
      growth), which fixes the order R5 iterates over those maps.
  JTS Topology Suite 1.13
      street/parkride.rs, street/geom.rs: the intersection of two polylines
      and the centroid of a polyline as R5 obtains them from JTS, and the
      fraction of a segment closest to a point.
  JTS Topology Suite 1.18.1 (the version in the R5 jar)
      itinerary/hops.rs: where a stop falls along a GTFS shape and the part
      of the shape between two such places (LocationIndexOfPoint,
      ExtractLineByLocation, as R5's TripPattern.getHopGeometries uses
      them); itinerary/wkt.rs: the WKT text WKTWriter writes for a line
      (OrdinateFormat: a DecimalFormat with pattern "0" and at most 16
      fraction digits).
      street/scenario/jts.rs: the STRtree spatial index (its query order),
      DiscreteHausdorffDistance between two lines and the predicate
      Polygon.intersects(LineString), which R5's RoadCongestion and
      ShapefileMatcher use for r5r's scenarios.
  java.text.DecimalFormat, java.text.DigitList and
  jdk.internal.math.FloatingDecimal (OpenJDK 21)
      itinerary/wkt.rs: the digits DecimalFormat writes for a double (the
      binary-to-decimal conversion of FloatingDecimal, which is not always
      the shortest, then rounded half-even to the fraction digits asked for
      with that conversion's exactness flags), which JTS's WKTWriter uses.
  libc++ std::unordered_map (LLVM)
      isochrone/isoband.rs (HashTable): the bucket counts, growth and node
      order of libc++'s hash table, which fix the order in which isoband
      returns the rings of an isoband when built on macOS.
  libstdc++ std::unordered_map (GCC)
      isochrone/isoband.rs (HashTable): the same for libstdc++'s hash
      table (GCC 11 to 16), which isoband uses when built on Linux and on
      Windows: the rehash policy's behaviour (_Prime_rehash_policy: 1
      bucket, 13 on the first key, then, when the size passes the bucket
      count, the policy's next count for twice the current one) and, in
      GNU_BUCKET_COUNTS, the 23 bucket counts (13 to 101,473,717) that this
      growth reaches for the tables isoband builds. These counts were
      recorded by calling the policy of the installed libstdc++; the
      library's table of primes itself is not included.
  s2geometry and the R package s2
      isochrone/edges.rs: the centroid of a line on the sphere as
      sf::st_centroid() obtains it through s2 (the sum of the segments'
      true centroids, normalised), and s2's conversion of longitudes and
      latitudes to unit vectors and back.

